The product rule is used to differentiate functions that can be written as the
product of two smaller functions.

Product rule

If f(x) = u(x) v(x), then\begin{align}
f' &= u v' + v u ' \\
&= u \dd{}{v}{x} + v \dd{}{u}{x}
\end{align}

To demonstrate this rule, consider f(x) = (x-3)(x-1).
Previously, we would have needed to expand this function before differentiating:\begin{align}
f(x) &= x^2 - 4x + 3 \\
f'(x) &= 2x - 4
\end{align}

With the product rule, instead we recognize\begin{align}
u &= x-3 &  v &= x-1 \\
u' &= 1 & v' &= 1
\end{align}

sof'(x) = uv' + vu' = (x-3) \cdot 1 + (x-1) \cdot 1 = 2x - 4

This example is a little silly, but the product rule is a life saver for
functions that are hard to expand! Here are some better examples:

f(x) = (x+1)(2x^2 + 5)(5x^3-4)

Identify:u = x+1  \qquad v = (2x^2+5)(5x^3-4)

sof'(x) = (x+1)v' + (2x^2 +3)(5x^3-4)(1)

The derivative v' can be evaluated by a second product rule!\begin{align}
u &= 2x^2+5 & v &= 5x^3-4 \\
u' &= 4x & v' &= 15x^2
\end{align}

finally givingmarkdownlint-disable MD011 \begin{align}
f'(x) &= (x+1)[(2x^2+3)(15x^2) + (5x^3-4)(4x)] \\
&+ (2x^2+5)(5x^3-4)
\end{align}markdownlint-enable MD011 

f(x) = \dfrac{1}{x} e^x

Identify:\begin{align}
u &= \frac{1}{x} & v &= e^x   \\
u' &= -\frac{1}{x^2} & v' &= e^x
\end{align}

so\begin{align}
f'(x) &= \frac{1}{x}e^x + e^x(-\frac{1}{x^2}) \\
&= e^x\left(\frac{1}{x} - \frac{1}{x^2}\right)
\end{align}

f(x) = (x^2+3)\ln x

Identify:\begin{align}
u &= x^2+3 &  v &= \ln(x)   \\
u' &= 2x & v' &= \frac{1}{x}
\end{align}

so\begin{align}
f'(x) &= (x^2+3)\cdot\frac{1}{x} + (\ln x)(2x) \\
&= \frac{x^2+3}{x} + 2x\ln x
\end{align}

Quotient Rule

If a function f(x) = u(x) / v(x) and v(x) \ne 0, then:f' = \frac{v u' - u v'}{v^2}

Hint

One mnemonic that may or may not help remember this:

If the quotient rule you wish to know, it’s “low-d-high less high-d-low”... then
draw the line and, down below, denominator squared will go.

To demonstrate this rule, consider f(x) = (x-1)/x. Previously, we would
have needed to separate the numerator before differentiating:\begin{align}
f(x) &= 1 - \frac{1}{x} \\
f'(x) &= \frac{1}{x^2}
\end{align}

With the qoutient rule, instead we recognize\begin{align}
u &= x - 1 & v &= x \\
u' &= 1 & v' &= 1
\end{align}

sof' = \frac{ x \cdot 1 - (x-1) \cdot 1}{x^2} = \frac{1}{x^2}

This was obviously harder than the old way, but there are other cases where it
is helpful to do the quotient rule! Here are some examples you can try:

\displaystyle f(x) = \frac{x^2 -1}{x^4 + 2}\begin{align}
u &= x^2 -1 & v &= x^4 +2 \\
u' &= 2x & v' &= 4x^3
\end{align}

so\begin{align}
f'(x) &= \frac{ (x^4 + 2) \cdot (2x) - (x^2 - 1) \cdot (4x^3)}{(x^4 +2)^2}\\
&= \frac{2x^5 + 4x^2 - 4x^5 +4x^3}{x^8 + 2x^4 + 4}
\end{align}

\displaystyle f(x) = \frac{e^{x}}{1 + x}\begin{align}
u &= e^{x} & v &= 1 + x \\
u' &= e^{x} & v' &= 1
\end{align}

so\begin{align}
f'(x) &= \frac{(1 + x) \cdot e^{x} - e^{x} \cdot 1}{(1 + x)^2} \\
&= \frac{x e^{x}}{(1 + x)^2}
\end{align}

\displaystyle f(x) = \frac{(x - 1)(x^2 - 2x)}{x^4}\begin{align}
u &=  & v &= x^4\\
u' &= 3x^2 - 6x + 2 & v' &= 4x^3 \\
\end{align}

so\begin{align}
f'(x) &= \frac{x^4 \cdot (3x^2-6x+2) - (x^3 -3x^2 +2x) \cdot 4x^3}{x^8} \\
    &= \frac{-x^6 + 6x^5 - 6x^4}{x^8} \\
    &= -\frac{1}{x^2} + \frac{6}{x^3} - \frac{6}{x^4}
\end{align}

Note, though, that in this case we could also have expanded the numerator,
divided through by x^8, and differentiated term-by-term to arrive at the
same answer. The faster route depends on the problem!

The chain rule is used to differentiate composite functions, when the “inside”
of the function is more complicated than what we know.

Chain rule

If a function f(x) can be written as f(u(x)), then:f' = \dd{}{f}{u} \dd{}{u}{x} = f'(u(x)) u'(x)

To demonstrate this rule, consider f(x) = (x-1)^2. Previously, we would have
needed to expand this function before differentiating:\begin{align}
f(x) &= x^2 - 2x + 1 \\
f'(x) &= 2x - 2
\end{align}

With the chain rule, instead we recognize\begin{align}
u &= x - 1  &  f &= u^2 \\
u' &= 1  &  f' &= 2u
\end{align}

sof'(x) = \dd{}{f}{u} \dd{}{u}{x} = 2u \dd{}{u}{x} = 2(x-1) \cdot 1 = 2x - 2

The results match! Some additional examples:

f(x) = e^{x^2}

Make the replacement u = x^2:\begin{align}
u &= x^2  &  f &= e^u \\
u' &= 2x  &  f' &= e^u
\end{align}

sof'(x) = \dd{}{f}{u} \dd{}{u}{x} = e^{u} \dd{}{u}{x} = e^{x^2} \cdot 2x

f(x) = \ln(1 + 2x)

Make the replacement u = 1+2x:\begin{align}
u &= 1 + 2x  & f &= \ln u \\
u' &= 2  &  f' &= \frac{1}{u}
\end{align}

sof'(x) = \dd{}{f}{u} \dd{}{u}{x} = \frac{1}{u} \dd{}{u}{x} = \frac{2}{1 + 2x}

f(x) = \dfrac{2}{1 + 2x}

Make the replacement u = 1+2x:\begin{align}
u &= 1 + 2x  & f &= 2u^{-1} \\
u' &= 2  &  f' &= -2u^{-2}
\end{align}

sof'(x) = \dd{}{f}{u} \dd{}{u}{x} = -2u^{-2} \cdot \dd{}{u}{x} =
    \frac{-4}{(1 + 2x)^2}

